# What is still TypeScript, and what it is waiting on

The language is Rust. Parsing, compiling and evaluating all happen in
`dsl-core` and `dsl-ir`, reached through `@asc/dsl-compiler` — natively on a
server, as WebAssembly in a browser. This page is the honest account of what
has not moved, because "mostly Rust" invites somebody to add to the wrong half.

## What moved

| What | Now answered by |
| --- | --- |
| Lexing, for editors | `lexExpr` — comments included, which the engine's own lexer used to discard |
| Program vs expression | `isProgramSource` — asked before every evaluation a form performs |
| Parse tree and diagnostics | `parse`, `diagnostics` |
| A path checked against a schema | `diagnosticsAgainstSchema` — the whole path, nested, not just its first segment |
| Stepping through an evaluation | `debugTrace` — every step with the source span it came from |

Two lexers is the failure this avoids. They had already drifted: the Rust one
discarded comments and had a `Regex` token, the TypeScript one emitted comments
and had none. An editor switched between them silently loses comment
highlighting, and a classifier that disagrees with the compiler about what a
keyword is sends a program down the expression path.

## What has not moved, and why

`src/expression/parser-detailed.ts` (2,192 lines) and `src/expression/sql.ts`
(2,111 lines) are still here, and the reason is a chain:

```
parser-detailed.ts  ←  sql.ts  ←  records-service search predicate
                              ←  schema designer expression editor
```

`sql.ts` compiles an expression to a SurrealQL condition so a search filters
in the DATABASE. That is not a convenience: without it a search fetches rows
and filters them here, after `LIMIT` has already been applied — so an entry
that matches but sorts below the limit is never found. It is checked against a
running SurrealDB by `conformance/surreal-parity.ts`, 26 of 26 translatable
cases answering what the engine answers, and what cannot be translated
faithfully is declined rather than guessed at.

So `sql.ts` is superseded in principle and load-bearing in practice. It was
deleted once on the strength of a search for its own path, which found only its
tests: both callers reach it through the package barrel. Typechecking the
APPLICATIONS, not the package, is what caught that.

## The two ways out

**Run the engine inside the database.** `crates/dsl-surrealism` compiles the
engine into a SurrealDB plugin, so `mod::asc::matches` answers what the
engine answers because it IS the engine — no translator, and the whole
language rather than the translatable subset. The search predicate becomes a
call rather than a compilation.

What is ready: the module source compiles. `bun run check:surrealism` in
`packages/dsl/core/rust` checks it against `wasm32-wasip1`, which is the
target it is built for - run from the crate's own directory, because it is a
separate workspace on purpose and cargo would otherwise resolve the engine's
and try to build host-only dependencies for wasm.

The engine also builds for that target: `cargo build --release --target
wasm32-wasip1` in the crate produces `dsl_surrealism.wasm`, 1.8 MB. So the
code is not the unknown here.

What remains: packaging and loading it. `bun run build:surrealism` runs
`surreal module build`, which exists in SurrealDB v3 and not in the v2 CLI
this machine has, and the artefact then has to reach the database:

```text
DEFINE BUCKET modules BACKEND "file:/modules";
DEFINE MODULE mod::asc AS f"modules:/asc-dsl.surli";
RETURN mod::asc::matches("root.age >= 65", { age: 74 });  -- true
```

`.surli` is a packaged component rather than that bare wasm - `surreal module
build` embeds `surrealism.toml`, including the capability grants, which is
the part that must not be guessed at. A module loaded with the wrong
capabilities is a security decision made by accident.

The migration that defines the module is deliberately NOT written yet: it
would fail against a database whose bucket has no artefact in it, and a
migration that cannot run blocks every migration behind it.

Loading it is an operator's action, not a build step. `DEFINE BUCKET` and
`DEFINE MODULE` change what a live database will execute, and the module's
grants are declared in `surrealism.toml` - nothing granted, deliberately.
That is a change somebody signs off, not one a tool applies on its way past.

**Port the translator to Rust.** Keeps the current shape and needs no
infrastructure, but it is 2,111 lines of translation whose correctness is
defined by behaviour against a live database, so the parity harness has to
move with it.

The first is smaller and is the architecturally right answer. The second is
available if the module cannot be deployed.

## Also still here

- `expression/lexer.ts` — the docs generator reads it, and `sql.ts` parses with it.
- `expression/ast.ts` — the language reference is generated by reading this
  file with ts-morph. Docs generated from the Rust definitions would retire it,
  and would make the types generated rather than mirrored by hand.
- `expression/js-comparison.ts` — not an evaluator. It compares two
  already-evaluated values while deciding whether a rule matched, for
  `unified-program.ts`.
